# The values `npm run xiai:dev` reads, as the shape of the file they go in.
#
#     cp worker/.dev.vars.example worker/.dev.vars
#
# `worker/.dev.vars` is ignored by git, and neither value belongs in the
# repository: the public key is not a secret but it does belong to one Discord
# application, and the webhook is a credential — anybody holding it can post to
# that channel.
#
# DISCORD_PUBLIC_KEY
#   Developer Portal → your application → General Information → Public Key.
#   Sixty-four hex characters. It is the verification half of the pair, and it is
#   the *only* key the deployed bot has: there is no bot token anywhere near the
#   endpoint, because verifying a signature and answering an interaction both
#   need something else — the application's public key, and the one-use token
#   Discord puts in the interaction itself.
#
# XIAI_FEEDBACK_WEBHOOK
#   Optional. A channel webhook the 👍/👎 buttons report through, which is the
#   only way the deployed bot can say anything on its own — `#mod-log` is the
#   intended home (Channel → Integrations → Webhooks → New Webhook → Copy
#   Webhook URL). Unset is a supported state: a vote is still acknowledged, it
#   just goes no further.
#
# The live Worker reads the same two names as Worker secrets, set once with
# `npx wrangler secret put DISCORD_PUBLIC_KEY --config worker/wrangler.toml`.
DISCORD_PUBLIC_KEY=
XIAI_FEEDBACK_WEBHOOK=
